WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.764
Before the next busy shift, your manager asks: do we need another cashier?

00:00:06.914 --> 00:00:13.267
Start with what actually happens at the checkout. Here is what your team should write down.

00:00:14.817 --> 00:00:20.886
Choose the line and the observation window. Here, it is nine to nine thirty.

00:00:21.036 --> 00:00:30.269
Use one clock, full dates, and anonymous customer identifiers. Arrival means joining this line, not entering the shop.

00:00:31.819 --> 00:00:37.692
Count everyone who joins during the whole window, not only completed transactions.

00:00:37.842 --> 00:00:45.005
In this teaching sample, twelve arrivals in thirty minutes gives lambda: twenty-four arrivals per hour.

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:55.897
Time the service itself. Leave out the customer's wait and the cashier's idle time.

00:00:56.047 --> 00:01:03.421
Suppose three completed services take one, two, and three minutes. Their average is two minutes.

00:01:03.571 --> 00:01:13.338
Sixty divided by two gives mu: thirty customers per hour, per counter. This is a rate estimate, not a guaranteed throughput.

00:01:14.888 --> 00:01:20.807
A busy checkout still counts as available. A cashier on break does not.

00:01:20.957 --> 00:01:29.320
If one of two checkouts closes at nine fifteen, c changes from two to one. Record when the change happens.

00:01:33.888 --> 00:01:39.582
Both samples average two minutes. Does that average tell the whole story?

00:01:42.888 --> 00:01:49.262
The second sample has short and long transactions. Variance describes that spread.

00:01:49.412 --> 00:01:58.010
Keep individual service times, not just their average. NovaQ expresses service-time variance in hours squared.

00:02:01.888 --> 00:02:06.687
Dana joins at nine oh five and leaves at nine ten because of the wait.

00:02:06.837 --> 00:02:15.435
Record both times and mark left before service. Her service never started, so do not make up service timestamps.

00:02:20.888 --> 00:02:27.382
A customer starts service after two minutes. Have we measured their maximum patience?

00:02:29.888 --> 00:02:34.587
No. We know they were willing to wait at least two minutes.

00:02:34.737 --> 00:02:40.736
If someone is still waiting when observation ends, their full patience is also unknown.

00:02:40.886 --> 00:02:49.199
A patience estimate needs served, left, and unfinished waits. Using only the people who left misses part of the picture.

00:02:51.888 --> 00:02:57.367
For an exponential-patience model, suppose mean patience is ten minutes.

00:02:57.517 --> 00:03:06.215
Theta is then six per hour for each waiting customer. It is not six percent leaving, or six total departures per hour.

00:03:06.365 --> 00:03:13.203
This assumed example is not an estimate from our small records. Theta is model-specific.

00:03:14.888 --> 00:03:22.682
Bring back arrivals, service starts and ends, customer and queue identifiers, and waiting outcomes.

00:03:22.832 --> 00:03:30.939
Also bring the observation window and a timed record of available checkouts and breaks. Keep unknown values unknown.

00:03:33.888 --> 00:03:44.016
NovaQ's completed-service event upload requires arrival, service start, and service end. Separate lines also need a queue identifier.

00:03:44.166 --> 00:03:52.579
Keep the full field log, including people who leave. Do not invent service times or quietly remove their arrivals.

00:03:52.729 --> 00:03:57.063
Use a supported input path and model for the records you have.

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:07.231
Lambda: arrivals. Mu: service speed per counter. C: available counters.

00:04:07.381 --> 00:04:14.835
Variance: how service times differ. Theta: a suitable patience model, when needed.

00:04:14.985 --> 00:04:20.388
Bring the observations, then examine the staffing question with the right model.
